// Two valid ways to detect a duplicate — different trade-offs.

// Option A: HashSet — O(n) time, O(n) extra space.
boolean hasDuplicateFast(int[] nums) {
    var seen = new HashSet<Integer>();
    for (int n : nums) {
        if (!seen.add(n)) return true; // add() returns false if n is already present
    }
    return false;
}

// Option B: sort then scan — O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space (in place).
boolean hasDuplicateLowMemory(int[] nums) {
    Arrays.sort(nums);                 // mutates input; ~n log n
    for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
        if (nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

Same result, opposite trade-off: pick Option A when speed matters and memory is free; pick Option B when memory is tight and you may mutate the input.

// Interviewer: Find the second largest element in an array

public int findSecondLargest(int[] nums) {
    // First attempt (with a bug):
    var largest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    var secondLargest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

    for (var num : nums) {
        if (num > largest) {
            secondLargest = largest; // Bug: not updating largest first
            largest = num;
        }
    }
    return secondLargest;
}

// Debugging narration:
// "Let me trace with input [3, 1, 4]. Expected: secondLargest is 3."
